## 1. Cloud Run dev/prod suffix — already implemented; I verified it rather than rebuilt it

This was done on 2026-09-13. Rather than take that on trust, I lifted the `resolve` job's shell verbatim into a throwaway git fixture and **executed all four cases**:

| Case | Result |
|---|---|
| commit on `main`, `PRODUCTION_BRANCH=main` | `prod` / contained |
| commit on a feature branch | `dev` / not-contained |
| `PRODUCTION_BRANCH` naming no branch | `dev` / no-branch |
| main commit, `origin` unreachable, ref in clone | `prod` (not demoted by the blip) |

The rule you stated — main is prod, everything else dev — is exactly the implemented default (`vars.PRODUCTION_BRANCH || 'main'`), and the suffix goes on the address's *local part* in `.github/actions/gcp-auth`, which also refuses a base that already carries one. **The one thing still outstanding is a setting, not code**: this repo has no `main` (branches are `development`/`master`), so on the default every release resolves to dev. It now warns loudly instead of doing that silently, but `vars.PRODUCTION_BRANCH` needs setting — and nothing in this sandbox can read or write repository variables.

## 2. Pending items by priority

plan.md's table is eight items: five blocked on a decision someone outside the code must make (palettes, the staff-page access question), two on hardware/GCP access, one deferred on its own trigger. I ran the board's own more reliable check — grepping for gaps the repo's *comments* admit to — with two subagents. That came back empty for stale comments, and the remaining admissions (mail/SMS/payment providers) are all account-blocked, confirming the board is honest.

But one item was real work and not on the table: **the render harness could not reach checkout past its first step**, recorded in three files' prose. I closed it.

- Fixtures now match on **method**, can be a **refusal**, and a **screen can carry its own** — checkout's two states disagree about one request (one needs `POST /orders` to succeed, the other needs it refused).
- `scope` separated from `expect`: these states *replace* the screen rather than covering it, so scoping to the marker would have measured a Callout and called the page under it clean.

**Result — the two states are geometrically clean** (no overflow, clipped text, misaligned rows, empty bands, or small targets at any of five widths). The 5 new contrast findings are all existing members of plan.md item (1); the sharpest is that `View order` and `Review new total` are both 3.80:1 white-on-rust — the same as `Place order`, so the primary action measures 3.80 in all three of its states.

Two things worth flagging:

- **Adding it found a defect in the harness itself.** The matcher keyed on path alone while `shopFixtures` answers `GET /api/v1/orders` with the order *list* — so the first POST this harness ever made was handed an `OrderSummary[]` as an `OrderDetail`. The fixture was right and the contract was right; the *matcher* was wrong, which is a door the typed fixtures don't watch.
- **I proved the assertion can fail** before trusting the clean result: breaking `Order placed`'s `expect` produced exactly 5 `reach opened nothing` findings, one per width.

## 3. Docs updated
plan.md (item 15, the three-dialogs section, the Cloud Run re-verification) and CLAUDE.md (§ "The render harness", § "State of the world" with the fourteenth run, § "What has never been run here").

**Gates:** typecheck, lint, format, build all pass; 5396 tests across 358 suites pass — matching the recorded totals exactly, so no count lines needed changing.

Two caveats, stated plainly: nothing here touched a database, Docker or GCP, so the Cloud Run path remains unexecuted end-to-end; and I've **left the changes uncommitted** since you didn't ask for a commit — say the word and I'll commit them to `development`.

