## What I did

**1. Cloud Run service-account suffix — already implemented; I verified it rather than rebuilding it.**

The `resolve` job plus `.github/actions/gcp-auth` already do exactly what you asked. I confirmed by execution against a throwaway git fixture, not by reading:

| Case | Result |
|---|---|
| tag cut from `main` | `prod` |
| tag cut from a feature branch | `dev` |
| `PRODUCTION_BRANCH` names no branch | `dev` + `::warning::` |
| main tag, `origin` unreachable | `prod` (not demoted by a blip) |

The suffix goes on the address's *local part* (`udaan-deployer-prod@…`), and it's on the Cloud Run services and Secret Manager entries too, not just the account.

**One thing to action on your side:** this repo has no `main` — branches are `development` and `master`. So on the default, every release resolves to `dev`. Set `vars.PRODUCTION_BRANCH` to `development`. There's no `gh` credential in this sandbox, so I can't set it from here.

**2. Pending items by priority.** The top four board items are all blocked on decisions or hardware, so I used the method plan.md records as having found real work 4/4 times — grepping for gaps the repo's own comments admit to. It hit again, in `AdminOrderDetailScreen.css`: *"this screen is not in the harness's list."* The desk's most-used drill-down had never been drawn by anything.

Adding it to the harness found a genuine defect on the first run: `order-return__credit-note` at **99 × 18**, the same control `apps/web` floored earlier — it survived because these sheets were *copied* into `apps/admin`, so improving the original never reached the twin. Neither workspace's sweep could see it: each finds its own rule exactly as written.

That composed with item (6), which turned out to be mis-filed. It's listed as "design judgement", but `design.html:239–322` draws the order drill-down *in full* — so it was transcription, not judgement. It's now the design's two-column grid; the other four drill-downs genuinely aren't drawn and stay judgement.

**3. plan.md and CLAUDE.md updated** in the same change, per the working agreement.

## Two judgement calls worth flagging

- **I kept the order number as the `<h1>`** where the design puts the retailer's name. The design carries the id in its own breadcrumb; this desk already draws one from the shell, and CLAUDE.md records the mono order number as one of three things making a drill-down read as a different screen. Taking the design's h1 meant a second breadcrumb or a lost one.
- **The rail is first in the DOM, second on screen.** Below 1024px this screen is a sales agent's, and lines-first would bury `Mark Packed` under a dozen of them.

## On verification

A clean sweep is also what a screen that never rendered produces, so I didn't rely on it. A probe confirms the grid computes to `695.109px 408.891px` at 1440 (the design's 1.7 : 1), rail at x=987, `sticky`; one column at 390. The two states draw different controls, proving the per-screen fixture answers. The four new spec rows were each checked against a deliberately broken copy — ten mutations, ten failures.

Worth knowing: my first round of break-tests was **unreliable** — `perl -i` was silently not writing on this filesystem, so one "pass" was really an unmade edit. I caught it and redid them with a method I verified. Had I not, I'd have reported a spec row as pinning something it didn't.

