💬 Done. Here's what happened.

## 1. Cloud Run env suffix — already implemented; verified, not rebuilt

This was done in two commits dated 2026-09-13/14. I verified it rather than duplicating it:

- **Branch → environment** (`resolve` job): `prod` if the commit is an *ancestor* of `vars.PRODUCTION_BRANCH` (default `main`), else `dev`. Containment rather than equality is deliberate — the trigger is a `v*` tag, and `refs/tags/v1.4.2` is never equal to `refs/heads/main`, so a name-equality rule would silently send every release to dev.
- **Suffix reaches every name**, which I checked line by line: the service account (`.github/actions/gcp-auth`, suffix on the local part), the migrate job, the API service, all three browser services, and all six Secret Manager entries. The image tag deliberately doesn't — same commit, same bytes.

**One thing to flag:** this repo has no `main` (branches are `development` and `master`). So on the default, *every* release resolves to dev and prod is unreachable. The workflow now warns about exactly this, but the fix is one repository-variable setting I can't make from here — it needs `vars.PRODUCTION_BRANCH` set to whichever branch you release from. That's board item (2), and it's a decision, not a task.

## 2. Pending items by priority

All eight ranked items are blocked on a decision, hardware, or GCP access. So I used the method plan.md says is its highest-yield (5 for 5): grep the repo for gaps its own comments admit to. That found one in the harness's own header — *"anything about a screen that an app's `screens` list does not name, **which is most of them**."*

The harness drew 13 of the desk's 36 routes. I added the four screens a shift actually starts from (ops home, fulfilment queue, retailer directory, products) plus brands, which came free — 6 fixtures, 5 screens.

**It found a real defect on the first run.** `DeskSearchField`'s 44px floor sat on the box, which carries `border: 1px` — so the content area was 42px and the stretched input took 42. Measured 310 × 42 at 390px on three screens, on the control the repo calls "the first thing a sales agent touches."

The interesting part is *why* it survived: `apps/web`'s twin has been correct since the ninth run and **names this file at its own declaration**. The padding half of the fix went desk→web; the floor half was worked out on web and never came back. Neither sweep could see it — each reads its own workspace, and an older twin contradicts nothing. Same class as the credit-note link the fifteenth run found, in the opposite direction. The desk's spec row was itself asserting the bug; it now makes `apps/web`'s assertion word for word, and I confirmed it fails against the old CSS before trusting it.

**What I deliberately did *not* fix:** ten remaining findings, all store/product names ellipsing. The repo's recorded rule is "whether the row says it again somewhere else," and all four tables have `rowHref` to a record that names the thing in full — so by the rule as written they're allowed. It also depends on how long real store names are, which nothing here can answer (no database has ever been touched). I put it on the board as item (17) with measurements and the two questions to settle, rather than re-laying-out three tables on my own fixture.

Verified: **target size 0 on both clients**, `apps/web` unchanged at its standing 257, fonts confirmed loaded so fit results are exact.

## 3. plan.md and CLAUDE.md updated

Board items (17) and (18) added, (19) closed, two new sections enumerating the 18 desk + 12 web screens still unnamed with what each costs. Worth knowing: the cheapest rows are the **eleven signed-out screens** across three apps that need no fixture at all — including the `/login` every operator and shopkeeper sees first, which nothing has ever drawn.

**All five gates pass** — typecheck, lint, format, test (5401/358, unchanged since I edited an existing spec rather than adding one), build. Nothing is committed; the working tree is yours to review.

